## We stopped firefighting this quarter

Three months ago every incident was a surprise. Today most of them are a procedure.

---
intent: problem
section: Where we were
notes: Do not soften it — June was bad, and everyone in the room remembers it.
---
## June was the worst month in two years

::: metrics
- 14 | incidents | Seven of them at night or on a weekend. {.hero}
- 41 min | average time to the first response
- 3 | customers who called the CEO
:::

---
intent: reveal
notes: The one number to take home. Pause on it.
---
## What changed

::: metrics
- 9 min | to the first response in September | From 41 in June, same team. {.hero}
- 5 | incidents | in September
- 0 | escalations to the CEO
:::

---
intent: prove
section: What we did
notes: The evidence, plain. Every number comes from the incident log.
---
## The playbooks are used, and they work

::: metrics
- 12 | playbooks written | One per recurring incident type.
- 87 % | of incidents matched a playbook | The other 13 % are now written up.
- 2 | drills run | July and September, whole team.
- 1 | tool | The crisis channel opens in one click.
:::

---
intent: explain
notes: Walk the steps — this is how an incident runs now, whoever is on call.
---
## How an incident runs today

::: cards
- Detect | The alert opens the crisis channel and pages the on-call.
- Qualify | The on-call picks the playbook in the first three minutes.
- Run | The playbook says who does what; the channel keeps the timeline.
- Close | A one-page review the next morning, and the playbook is updated.
:::

---
intent: decide
section: What is next
notes: Two ways to cover nights. I recommend the rotation, and here is why.
---
## Nights: two options

::: compare
### Outsource the night shift
- Coverage from day one.
- No context on our systems: every night call escalates to us anyway.
- 18 k€ a year.
### A paid on-call rotation {.hero}
- Six volunteers, one week in six.
- The people who wrote the playbooks answer the phone.
- 11 k€ a year, and the knowledge stays in the team.
:::

---
intent: celebrate
notes: Names, out loud. Then applause.
---
## Thank you, night owls

Malik, Sarah, Jonas, Priya, Tom and Léa took every night call in September — and wrote the playbooks that mean fewer calls next month.

